## Notes

- Never compare float arrays with `==`, and never assert `(a == b).all()`: a difference in the last bit fails the test, and the failure shows no values.
- `pytest.approx` is for scalars and short lists; it does not say which element differed and checks neither dtype nor shape (pytest-assertions in python-testing covers scalar assertions).
{% if has_ml_dep("numpy") %}
- `np.testing.assert_allclose(actual, desired, rtol=..., atol=...)`: set `atol` whenever the expected values include zeros, since a relative tolerance alone allows no difference at all at zero. Use `np.testing.assert_array_equal` for integers, booleans and strings; both treat missing values in the same position as equal.
{% endif %}
{% if has_ml_dep("pandas") %}
- pandas: `pd.testing.assert_frame_equal` and `assert_series_equal` check the index, dtypes and column order by default. Loosen `check_dtype`, `check_like` (which ignores column order) or `check_exact` only with a comment saying why.
{% endif %}
{% if has_ml_dep("polars") %}
- polars: `polars.testing.assert_frame_equal` and `assert_series_equal` check dtypes and order by default; loosen a check only with a comment saying why.
{% endif %}
- Build expected data inline from small cases computed by hand (three sensors over two days), so a reader can see why each number is right, or with a factory fixture (pytest-factory-fixtures in python-testing). Never load it from golden CSV files: a CSV round trip loses dtypes and index names, and nobody checks a thousand rows by eye.
